const DECOMPOSE: DecomposeSpec = {
  big:
    'The starter\'s `GameManager.cs` declares six lists and hands you a deal that is only half a ' +
    'deal: `Shuffle` has two empty overloads, and `Deal()` reads `player_deck[0]` and ' +
    '`ai_deck[0]` and never once calls `Add` on `player_hand` or `ai_hand`. So the moment a deal ' +
    'finishes, both hands are still empty lists — and a loop over an empty list runs zero times ' +
    'and reports nothing at all. "How do I hold a hand of cards?" is not one problem. It is six, ' +
    'and each one is a single line: make the list exist, put a card in, read one back by index, ' +
    'take one out, ask how many are left, and walk the lot with a loop.',
  frame: 'concept',
  parts: [
    {
      name: 'Make the list exist: empty, before anything goes into it',
      does:
        '`List<Card> player_hand = new List<Card>();` — declared once at the top of the class, and it starts with zero items. Zero is a legal state, not a broken one: an empty list answers `Count` with 0, and a loop over it simply runs no times, in silence.',
      doc:
        'the "Array vs List — same idea, one difference that matters" section of the Lists & Arrays reference, and the six list fields declared at the top of `Assets/Scripts/GameManager.cs`',
      href: '../docs/unit-10/lists-arrays.html',
    },
    {
      name: 'Put a card into it: APPEND',
      does:
        '`player_hand.Add(card)` puts one card on the end and makes the list one longer. AP writes the same operation `APPEND(aList, item)` — one item, one end, no index to get wrong and nothing to resize by hand.',
      doc:
        'the APPEND row of the AP-pseudocode-versus-C# table in the Lists & Arrays reference',
      href: '../docs/unit-10/lists-arrays.html',
    },
    {
      name: 'Read one card back out: the index',
      does:
        '`player_hand[0]` is the first card and `player_hand[Count - 1]` is the last. C# counts from 0 and the exam counts from 1, so the same card is `hand[3]` to you and item 4 to the exam — write both numbers down.',
      doc:
        'the `aList[i]` row of that table, and "Bug 2 — the bounds", which is the crash that comes from reading one index too far',
      href: '../docs/unit-10/lists-arrays.html',
    },
    {
      name: 'Take a card back out: REMOVE',
      does:
        '`player_hand.RemoveAt(i)` deletes the card at index i and shifts every later card down one. AP calls it `REMOVE(aList, i)`. That shift is the whole reason removing while looping forward skips a card.',
      doc:
        'the REMOVE row of that table, and "Bug 1 — remove while traversing forward", the bug the shift causes',
      href: '../docs/unit-10/lists-arrays.html',
    },
    {
      name: 'Ask how many are left: LENGTH',
      does:
        '`player_hand.Count` is a property rather than a method, and it is the number of cards right now rather than a total you have to remember to keep updated. AP writes it `LENGTH(aList)`, and it is the bound the exam puts in every list loop.',
      doc:
        'the LENGTH row of that table, and the `Count` bound in "Bug 2 — the bounds"',
      href: '../docs/unit-10/lists-arrays.html',
    },
    {
      name: 'Walk the whole hand: a loop over the list',
      does:
        '`foreach (Card c in player_hand)` runs the body once per card, in order, with no index to keep. That is "traverse to total" and "traverse with a filter" from the reference, pointed at a hand instead of a spreadsheet column.',
      doc:
        'patterns 1 and 2, "Traverse to total" and "Traverse with a filter", in the Lists & Arrays reference, and the `foreach` you ran over an area query in 8.3',
      href: '8.3.html',
    },
  ],
  why:
    'The split is the lesson, and its shape is the giveaway: five of these six parts are a single ' +
    'line of C#. "Hold a hand of cards" sounds like a system; it is four list operations, one ' +
    'index and one loop, and the Unit 10 reference prints every one of them with its AP pseudocode ' +
    'beside it. The reason to write the split down anyway is what the starter has not done: the ' +
    'fields are declared, `Shuffle` is empty, and nothing has ever been added to a hand — so the ' +
    'honest answer to "where is my hand" is "nowhere yet", and knowing which of the six small ' +
    'things is missing is the whole job today.',
  sizeTest:
    'The test for a finished split: can you look one part up in a single search, right now, and ' +
    'write it in one line? "How do I add a card to a list" can — the reference sheet prints the C# ' +
    'and the AP side by side. "How do I hold a hand of cards" cannot, because it is six of those ' +
    'questions wearing one name. A part that needs you to design something before you can write it ' +
    'is not a part of this problem; it is the next one.',
  turns: [
    '`Deal()` shuffles both decks, instantiates a card visual for each side and reads `player_deck[0]` and `ai_deck[0]` — and never once calls `Add` on `player_hand` or `ai_hand`. So the moment a deal finishes, both hands are still empty lists, and a loop over either one runs zero times and prints nothing. Where does the first `Add` go in your copy, and what would you print to prove the hand is no longer empty?',
    'The decks and the hands do not hold the same type: `player_deck` and `ai_deck` are `List<Card_data>` — an asset, the card\'s numbers — while the hands and the two discard piles are `List<Card>`, the component sitting on a card object in the scene. Why does the deck want the asset type rather than the scene type, and what would you lose if you shuffled the visual objects instead?',
    'This jam has five classes to ship and six lists to fill. Which of the six parts above would you write first if you wanted a card visible on screen inside ten minutes — and which one can wait until you have decided the rules of your game?',
  ],
};

const body = `<h1>10.2a — Card Jam Kickoff: Lists &amp; Arrays</h1>

<p class="lesson-lead">
    Second jam, new data structure: the LIST. Your coins were one
    number, your inventory was a few bools — a deck of cards is
    MANY things, in order, changing. The starter ships a card game
    skeleton (GameManager.cs) with a secret: two of its methods are
    EMPTY, waiting for you.
</p>

${renderAskBanner('how do I use lists to hold a hand of cards?', 3, 'concept')}

${renderDecompose('10.2a', DECOMPOSE, 3)}

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="activity rpg">
    <div class="activity-header">
        <span class="activity-label">Concept Warmup</span>
        <span class="activity-time">~15 min</span>
    </div>

    <p><strong>Open the starter's GameManager.cs</strong> (find it
    in the project — the card game scene). Two facts to verify with
    your own eyes:</p>
    <ol>
        <li>It is a singleton — <code>public static GameManager gm;</code>
            set in Awake, behind a duplicate guard and a
            <code>DontDestroyOnLoad</code>. That is the shape you
            built yourself in <a href="5.3.html">5.3</a>; the
            <code>StatManager</code> stub you met in
            <a href="2.1.html">2.1</a> was left empty on purpose and
            only got filled in during that refactor, so this pattern
            is yours. The game manager is THE manager; everything
            finds it.</li>
        <li>It holds exactly SIX Lists, and they are not all the same
            kind. Count them: TWO decks — <code>List&lt;Card_data&gt;</code>,
            the asset, the card's numbers — and TWO hands plus TWO
            discard piles, all <code>List&lt;Card&gt;</code>, the
            component on a card object. There is no play field and no
            score list; six is the whole inventory. THE WHOLE GAME IS
            LISTS.</li>
    </ol>

    <p><strong>List vocabulary (the exam's, exactly):</strong></p>
    <table>
        <tr><th>Exam pseudocode</th><th>C# on a List</th><th>What it does</th></tr>
        <tr><td>aList[i]</td><td>hand[i]</td><td>Access the item at index i (starts at 1 in AP, 0 in C#!)</td></tr>
        <tr><td>APPEND(aList, item)</td><td>hand.Add(card)</td><td>Add at the end</td></tr>
        <tr><td>REMOVE(aList, i)</td><td>hand.RemoveAt(i)</td><td>Remove item at index i</td></tr>
        <tr><td>INSERT(aList, i, item)</td><td>hand.Insert(i, card)</td><td>Squeeze into position i</td></tr>
        <tr><td>LENGTH(aList)</td><td>hand.Count</td><td>How many items</td></tr>
    </table>

    <div class="socratic">
        <h3>Socratic</h3>
        <p>
            Your deck has 30 cards. You draw the top one: deck[0],
            REMOVE it, APPEND it to the hand. Why must the removal
            happen — what breaks at draw #31 if you never remove?
            (This is also why your coins could never be a List of
            coin objects — count what you keep, list what you
            ORDER.)
        </p>
    </div>
</div>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="activity rpg">
    <div class="activity-header">
        <span class="activity-label">Pitch</span>
        <span class="activity-time">~25 min</span>
    </div>

    <p><strong>The jam task, read carefully:</strong> finish the
    starter's card game as YOUR version — your rules, your theme,
    your art swap — and ship it in five classes
